{"availability":"available","full":"Riteshbhandarii/Chess-engine","name":"Chess-engine","description":"TEORIAT Chess Engine is a personalized chess AI trained on my complete Chess.com history using a sequential PyTorch model that encodes each move as a compact tuple (color, move id, TEORIAT flag) and predicts the next move from the learned move vocabulary, imitating my real playing style rather than optimal engine play.","category":"Education","language":"Jupyter Notebook","stars":1,"forks":0,"created":"2025-03-18T10:37:15Z","updated":"2026-10-08T21:11:14Z","branch":"main","homepage":"https://chess-engine-two.vercel.app","topics":[],"demo":"https://chess-engine-two.vercel.app/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:12.284Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:12.284Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-08T23:43:12.284Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-08T23:43:12.284Z","stars":1,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["TEORIAT is a chess bot trained to imitate one player: the Chess.com account teoriat. It does not try to play the best move. A small recurrent network predicts the move that player would likely make, a set of chess heuristics filters out the worst blunders, and the result is served through a web app you can play against.","src/tables.py downloads the teoriat game archives from the Chess.com public API (January 2023 to August 2025) into PostgreSQL. The training set exported from that database, cleaneddata.csv, holds 1,000 games and 60,600 move-prediction examples.","A PyTorch GRU is trained on those games to predict the next move from the last six plies. At play time a FastAPI backend combines the network's candidates with simple tactical checks and picks a move. A React frontend provides the board, clocks, move list and a leaderboard."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Riteshbhandarii/Chess-engine/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"14256c38c6007b89113202545eda8dcffdb56da8515801e419